ENVIRONMENTAL NOTE — recorded, not hidden
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
The runs above were made with a git author/committer identity present in the
environment:

  GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=Test GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=t@example.com
  GIT_COMMITTER_NAME=Test GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL=t@example.com

Without it, `bash tests/component-path-diff-basis.tests.sh` (T-002) ABORTS on
this host with exit 128 and no `Results:` line:

    fatal: unable to auto-detect email address
    (got 'jrmag@02-C1-50-65-F3-3D.(none)')

Cause, established by reading the suite: its `git_repo_init` helper (line 39)
does set `user.email`/`user.name` on every repo it creates, but TEST-024 case 2
(line ~331) commits inside the SUBMODULE working tree `$R24/vendor/inner`,
which `git submodule add` cloned fresh and which therefore carries no identity.
This host has a repo-local `user.email` but NO global one, so git falls back to
hostname auto-detection and refuses.

This is pre-existing and environmental, not a regression from any work in this
session: `git log -- tests/component-path-diff-basis.tests.sh` shows the file
was last touched at `83561379`, and it is unchanged across `3919eddd..HEAD`.
The PowerShell twin passes 18/0 on the same host, so the two twins are not
environment-equivalent. Filed as a follow-up; the fix is to set an identity on
the submodule working tree before committing in it.
